1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,460
In this animation starts with a Python file,

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:04,620
a title appears, a circle moves across the

3
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:06,980
screen and both fade away. A few lines

4
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:10,310
describe the visuals and they're timing. FacelessChamp turns

5
00:00:10,310 --> 00:00:12,800
those instructions into a video. In this quick

6
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:15,780
start, we'll build that animation from scratch, render

7
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,790
our first MP4, and see how the same

8
00:00:17,790 --> 00:00:21,040
approach extends to narrated explainers and larger projects.

9
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:24,580
FacelessChamp is a Python library for creating animated

10
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:27,380
videos. You describe what should appear, where it

11
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:29,870
belongs, and how it changes over time. The

12
00:00:29,870 --> 00:00:32,940
core ideas are scenes, components, and animations. A

13
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:35,680
scene holds the visual content and its timeline.

14
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,000
Components are the things you place inside it.

15
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,630
Text, shapes, images, charts, or diagrams. Animations describe

16
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:45,360
changes to those components, such as fading in,

17
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:48,520
moving, or revealing text. Let's start with the

18
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:52,240
setup. You'll need Python version 3.12 or newer,

19
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:56,190
Git, and FFmpeg. FFmpeg handles video encoding, and

20
00:00:56,190 --> 00:00:58,590
its installation needs to include FF probe and

21
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:03,730
the H. 264 encoder called Libex, 264. Clone

22
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:06,030
the repository and open its folder. Create a

23
00:01:06,030 --> 00:01:09,140
Python virtual environment, activate it, and install the

24
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:11,360
library from the checkout using the command on

25
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:13,930
screen. This gives us an isolated environment for

26
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:16,910
the project, and makes the FacelessChamp render command

27
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:20,690
available. Now create a file called hello.py. At

28
00:01:20,690 --> 00:01:22,910
the top, we import the tools we need,

29
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:27,360
canvas, scene, text, circle, and three animation effects.

30
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:29,710
Next, we define a class called hello that

31
00:01:29,710 --> 00:01:32,240
inherits from scene. This is our first scene.

32
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:35,310
The initializer sets up a black canvas, 1920

33
00:01:35,310 --> 00:01:38,270
pixels wide, and 1080 pixels tall. These are

34
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:40,890
design coordinates. When we export it to lower

35
00:01:40,890 --> 00:01:43,990
resolution, the framework scales the visuals with the

36
00:01:43,990 --> 00:01:46,990
canvas. The construct method describes what happens in

37
00:01:46,990 --> 00:01:49,360
the scene. First, we create a title that

38
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:52,140
says hello, FacelessChamp. We give it a font

39
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:55,140
size, a white color, and a position. Positions

40
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:58,880
use two numbers, horizontal position, then vertical position.

41
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:01,240
The origin is the top left corner. Moving

42
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:04,380
right increases the first number. Moving down increases

43
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:07,100
the second. Components use a center anchor by

44
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:10,110
default, so our titles position places its center

45
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:13,420
at 960 across and 400 down. Next, we

46
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:16,580
create a turquoise circle. Its radius is 60

47
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:19,200
design pixels, and its starting position is near

48
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,580
the lower left side of the canvas. We've

49
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:23,970
described our objects. Now let's put them on

50
00:02:23,970 --> 00:02:26,360
the timeline. Our first play call combines a

51
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:28,570
typewriter reveal for the title, with a fade

52
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:31,070
in for the circle. Because both animations are

53
00:02:31,070 --> 00:02:33,520
inside the same play call, they happen together.

54
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:36,100
The runtime argument gives this step a duration

55
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:37,750
of one and a half seconds, and we

56
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:39,790
wait for half a second, giving the viewer

57
00:02:39,790 --> 00:02:41,980
time to read. The next play call moves

58
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:44,120
the circle to the right side of the

59
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:47,520
canvas. The animate builder describes the target position,

60
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:50,130
and runtime controls how long the movement takes,

61
00:02:50,130 --> 00:02:53,390
separate play calls happen in sequence. Animations inside

62
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:56,230
one play call happen together, a wait creates

63
00:02:56,230 --> 00:02:58,520
a pause. Those three rules are enough to

64
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:01,650
build a simple timeline. Finally, we pause, fade

65
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:04,090
out both objects, and hold the empty canvas

66
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:05,990
for a moment, save the file, and run

67
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:08,440
the render command shown here. We pass the

68
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:11,100
python file name, the scene class name, and

69
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:13,940
an output path. The low quality preset makes

70
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:16,690
a smaller preview, and we use lower anti-ilising

71
00:03:16,690 --> 00:03:20,080
for this first check. Open the exported MP4.

72
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:22,430
You should see the title appear, the circle

73
00:03:22,430 --> 00:03:25,520
move, and both disappear. Try changing one thing.

74
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:28,270
Replace the title, choose another circle color, or

75
00:03:28,270 --> 00:03:31,010
increase the movement duration. Render again with the

76
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:32,760
overwrite flag when you want to replace the

77
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,500
existing output. Now the connection is visible. A

78
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:37,280
change in the code becomes a change in

79
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:39,670
the video. For longer videos, the same scene

80
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:43,680
model supports imported images, audio, and subtitle timing.

81
00:03:43,740 --> 00:03:46,060
You can attach an existing voiceover at time

82
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:48,810
zero, load its srt file, and schedule a

83
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:51,420
visual at a subtitle queue. With word level

84
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:54,020
timestamps, this lets a diagram appear when the

85
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:56,790
narration introduces it. That makes the framework useful

86
00:03:56,790 --> 00:03:59,840
for math explainers, programming lessons, and system design

87
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:03,200
videos. When a project needs more structure, faceless

88
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:06,410
champ kit adds named segments, reusable blocks, themes,

89
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:09,090
asset management, and workflow commands on top of

90
00:04:09,090 --> 00:04:12,000
the core engine. Install the bundled kit, create

91
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,540
a project from the silent template, and inspect

92
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:17,190
the generated video file. Run Doctor to check

93
00:04:17,190 --> 00:04:19,790
the environment, validate to check the video, and

94
00:04:19,790 --> 00:04:22,260
preview to export a short section. Once the

95
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:25,140
preview looks right, render the complete video. For

96
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:27,860
your first project, keep the scope small, one

97
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:30,850
title, one visual, and one clear action. Build

98
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:33,180
the scene, check a short preview, and refine

99
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:35,800
the timing. Then add narration and more scenes

100
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:38,380
as the idea grows. That's your first faceless

101
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:41,450
champ workflow. Describe the visuals in Python, animate

102
00:04:41,450 --> 00:04:44,540
them on a timeline, and render the result

103
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:46,080
into a video.

